INT. CAR WASH TUNNEL - NIGHT

Dark. Foam sluices over the windshield. Spinning brushes. Sodium light bleeds through soap in slow orange waves.

ODALYS FERRERA, 40s, sits behind the wheel of a dented sedan, both hands on ten and two. Funeral dress. Hair still pinned.

ODALYS (V.O.)
Forty-one years. He never once washed the car.

The brushes slap the roof. She doesn't flinch.

ODALYS (V.O.) (CONT'D)
Said it would just get dirty again.

She switches off the radio. The tunnel roars.

ODALYS (V.O.) (CONT'D)
So I let it.

Rinse arches fire. Water drums the glass. She leans her forehead on the wheel.

ODALYS (V.O.) (CONT'D)
Now I'm paying six dollars to sit in here.

The dryer blasts. The car shudders. She starts laughing. Then she stops.

She puts the car in drive. Rolls into the light.
